Context. This is the 'variations sur l'équation des lacets' part of the sequel to La Longue Marche (pp. 293–650). Grothendieck is analysing a family of algebraic group schemes M_{ρ,σ} that come with two maps:
- a homomorphism Θ: M_{ρ,σ} → G, where G is a GL_2-type group with SG = SL_2 and PG = PGL_2;
- a character χ = det∘Θ: M_{ρ,σ} → G_m.
Inside M_{ρ,σ} sit the following subgroups:
- tori T_ρ, T_σ and their determinant-one parts ST_ρ, ST_σ;
- a normalizer-type group N'_σ;
- a 'Galois/fibre' subgroup 𝔊^+_{ρ,σ}, identified with SG;
- the kernel R_{ρ,σ} = Ker Θ̃, where Θ̃ is the composite M → G → PG, together with its unimodular part SR_{ρ,σ} = Ker Θ and the connected component R°.

Previous page. Grothendieck showed:
- SR ∩ SG = {1}, because Θ restricted to SG is the inclusion SG ⊂ G;
- R ∩ SG = Ker(SG → PG) = μ_2;
- (98): SR → T^+_{ρ,σ} ≅ S'T_ρ × N'_σ is an isomorphism, and so is SR° → ST_ρ × ST_σ. The second comes from comparing earlier formulas; the first then follows by the five lemma, since both quotients by connected components are μ_2 × μ_2.

This page.
1. Besides the obvious splitting of M^+ (the kernel of χ) as a semidirect product M^+(0)·SG, there is a second splitting 'linked much more naturally to the very structure' of M (via Θ and χ). It is given by the section subgroup SR, and it makes M^+ a direct product, (99): M^+ ≅ SR × SG ≅ (S'T_ρ × SN'_σ) × SG.
2. Restricting to identity components gives (100): M^{+°} ≅ SR° × SG ≅ ST_ρ × ST_σ × SG. A marginal note says this is formula (46), forgotten along the way ('qui dit mieux !').
3. On the full group, the map R_{ρ,σ} → T_{ρ,σ} is only an isogeny (underlined in the image), not an isomorphism. So up to that isogeny one has a 'section' of M itself, viewed as an extension of T_{ρ,σ} by 𝔊^+ ≅ SG.
4. (101) records two facts:
- R ∩ SG = μ_2;
- R commutes with SG. This is trivial because R = Ker Θ̃ maps to the centre of G.
   A struck line had wrongly claimed that R no longer commutes, so that there is no exact sequence.
5. Hence the next page's exact sequence (102): 1 → μ_2 → R × SG → M → 1, with ε ↦ (ε, ε^{-1}). In words, M is the central product R ×^{μ_2} SG.

Next page. Grothendieck makes R explicit as a fibre product of T_ρ, N'_σ and G_m over determinants, and gives the cartesian square (105): R → T_{ρ,σ} over the squaring map δ²: G_m → G_m.

Purpose. The aim is a clean structural description of the groups M_{ρ,σ}, which serve as symmetry groups attached to the loop equation (the Grothendieck–Teichmüller-type 'équation des lacets'). Concretely, M is shown to be a central product of a torus-like group with SL_2.
